Chapter: BpSymbolicWriteTest
  cites Works chapter DevDebugger
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The WRITE half of symbolic breakpoints: `bp-add-symbolic` resolves a name
 through MAP1 and then puts 0xCC over the first byte of that function.
 `codex/test/apps/map1-lookup` covers the read half and asserts no address
 or name, because the map is a map OF the test binary.

 THE HAZARD IS THAT THE TARGET IS THIS BINARY. A test that breakpoints a
 function it then calls patches its own running code and traps. So the
 victim is a function defined here that is called exactly once, BEFORE the
 breakpoint is set, and never again: the call is what keeps it live and in
 the map, and everything after the poke is arithmetic and peeks. The 0xCC
 is in place for the span of one `peek-byte` and is restored in the same
 breath.

 NO ADDRESS OR COUNT IS ASSERTED, for map1-lookup's reason. What is
 asserted is the cycle: the byte is 0xCC after the set, the byte the
 debugger recorded is the one that was there before, and the byte is back
 after the remove. A restore that wrote the wrong value would pass a test
 that only checked "not 0xCC".

 THE ENTRY COUNT IS TAKEN BEFORE THE REMOVE, and the ordering is
 load-bearing rather than stylistic. `bp-remove` rebuilds the table, but
 the list the OLD table points at does not survive it: reading
 `d1.breakpoints.entries` after the remove answers 0, and it answers 0 for
 the state that still reports `next-id: 2` and a status naming the
 breakpoint it set. Measured that way this test said the add had never
 happened while every other line said it had.

 THE MISS IS THE CONTROL, twice over. `zzz-no-such-symbol` must leave the
 table empty AND must not poke anything -- a resolver that fell through to
 address 0x100000 would corrupt the image while reporting failure.

 We say:

Section: The Victim

 IT HAS TO SURVIVE INLINING OR IT IS NOT IN THE MAP AT ALL. The first
 version of this was `bp-victim (x) = x * 3 + 1`, called once with a
 literal; the compiler folded it away and `map1-find-by-name` answered None,
 which reads exactly like a broken resolver. Self-recursion is what keeps it
 a real function with a real address. User functions ARE in MAP1 -- this
 test's own `opening` and `cycle-report` are in there -- so an absent symbol
 means it was optimised out, not that the map omits user code.

  bp-victim : Integer -> Integer
  bp-victim (n) = if n <= 0 then 4 else bp-victim (n - 1)

Section: The Cycle

  cycle-report : Map1Info, Map1Entry, Integer -> Text
  cycle-report (m) (entry) (warm) =
    let addr = 1048576 + entry.code-offset
    in let before = peek-byte addr 0
    in let d1 = bp-add-symbolic debugger-init "bp-victim"
    in let after-set = peek-byte addr 0
    in let recorded = recorded-byte (d1.breakpoints)
    in let count-set = list-length (d1.breakpoints.entries)
    in let d2 = __record-set d1 "breakpoints" (bp-remove (d1.breakpoints) 1)
    in let after-remove = peek-byte addr 0
    in let count-removed = list-length (d2.breakpoints.entries)
    in let a = "set-wrote-int3: " & show (after-set == 204)
    in let b = "recorded-original: " & show (recorded == before)
    in let c = "remove-restored: " & show (after-remove == before)
    in let d = "one-entry-after-set: " & show (count-set == 1)
    in let e = "removed-empty: " & show (count-removed == 0)
    in let f = "warm-called: " & show (warm == 4)
    in a & "\n" & b & "\n" & c & "\n" & d & "\n" & e & "\n" & f

  recorded-byte : BreakpointTable -> Integer
  recorded-byte (tbl) =
    if list-length (tbl.entries) == 0 then 0 - 1
    else (list-at (tbl.entries) 0).original-byte

Section: The Control

 A name in no binary. The table must stay empty, and the status must say so
 rather than reporting a breakpoint at some resolved-by-accident address.

  miss-report : Text
  miss-report =
    let d = bp-add-symbolic debugger-init "zzz-no-such-symbol"
    in "miss-table-empty: " & show (list-length (d.breakpoints.entries) == 0)

Section: Body

  report-text : Integer -> Text
  report-text (warm) =
    when map1-find
      is None -> "map1: NOT FOUND"
      is Just (m) -> named-report m warm

  named-report : Map1Info, Integer -> Text
  named-report (m) (warm) =
    when map1-find-by-name m "bp-victim"
      is None -> "victim: NOT IN MAP"
      is Just (entry) -> "map1: FOUND\n" & cycle-report m entry warm & "\n" & miss-report

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (report-text (bp-victim 1))
  end

